  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 385e88b7439744b4db7315d03e5de07f, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::RangerMenuUI
  menuRoot: {fileID: 54056378947635981}
  mainMenuPanel: {fileID: 6832780504721083351}
  responseText: {fileID: 7412834449272236582}
  customQuestionPanel: {fileID: 3701119819432586163}
  questionInput: {fileID: 7705055677673275981}
  characterScript: {fileID: 0}
  cameraScript: {fileID: 0}
  defaultMessage: Ask me about Yellowstone in the Climate Change Era, from the 2000s
    to today.
  eraName: "Climate Change Era (2000s\u2013today)"
  historyResponse: You are visiting Yellowstone during the Climate Change Era. In
    this period, the park is being shaped not only by tourism and conservation, but
    also by environmental change on a large scale. Yellowstone has faced longer wildfire
    seasons, changing weather patterns, and more visible climate-related impacts
    across the landscape. Park management today must respond to these challenges
    while still protecting wildlife, preserving natural features, and maintaining
    access for visitors. This era reflects a balance between conservation, science,
    and adaptation to a changing environment.
  wildlifeResponse: Wildlife in Yellowstone during the Climate Change Era reflects
    both recovery and new challenges. One major success is the wolf reintroduction,
    which helped restore an important predator to the ecosystem. At the same time,
    longer wildfire seasons and changing environmental conditions affect habitats
    and animal movement. Wildlife here continues to adapt to a landscape shaped by
    fire, climate pressure, and human management. Yellowstone remains one of the
    most important places in the country for observing how ecosystems respond to
    change over time.
  buildingsResponse: "Buildings and infrastructure in Yellowstone\u2019s Climate
    Change Era are increasingly affected by environmental damage. Heavy rain, mudslides,
    and related events can damage roads and disrupt travel through the park. Construction
    and maintenance in this era are focused not just on visitor access, but also
    on repairing and adapting infrastructure to withstand changing conditions. Roads,
    drainage systems, and other built features must now respond to a landscape that
    is becoming harder to predict because of climate change."
  rangerApiUrl: http://localhost:3000/ask-ranger
